omr-server (0.1085) unstable; urgency=medium

    * shadowsocks.conf, shadowsocks.6.1.conf, shadowsocks.6.18.conf: net.ipv4.tcp_mtu_probing 0 -> 1 (the comment above it said "turn on path MTU discovery"). 1 is blackhole detection: after a retransmission timeout the socket shrinks its segment size and probes back up, the fallback for paths that filter ICMP "fragmentation needed" (LTE/5G, PPPoE, CGNAT); healthy paths pay nothing. Proven on the bench with one MPTCP download over a healthy path and a path whose 1300-MTU hop drops its frag-needed messages, 100 Mbit/s each: with 0 the second subflow stayed at 1448-byte segments, delivered nothing, was marked stale and never recovered, so the connection ran at 85-90 Mbit/s on one WAN; with 1 it came back at 1214-byte segments and the connection ran at 152-155 Mbit/s (three runs each). The VPS matters as much as the router here: it sends the downloads. Not 2, which starts every connection at 1024-byte segments
    * openvpn-tun0.6.1.conf, openvpn-tun0.6.1-ipv6.conf: stop pinning the TCP tunnel's socket buffers. "sndbuf 262144"/"rcvbuf 262144", also pushed to every router, set SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF, which the kernel doubles to 512 KB and which turns TCP autotuning off, so the tunnel's window could never pass 512 KB: about 200 Mbit/s at 20 ms RTT and 80 Mbit/s at 50 ms per tunnel, whatever the links. Both are now 0 and nothing is pushed, so the socket starts at tcp_rmem's default and grows up to the 128 MB maximum ("Socket Buffers: R=[87380->87380]" on the bench VPS, i.e. left alone)
    * openvpn-tun1.6.1.conf: UDP never autotunes, so the UDP tunnel keeps an explicit buffer, raised from 262144 to 4194304 (8 MB once doubled, ~6 ms at 10 Gbit/s; unset it would have fallen back to rmem_default, 208 KB) and still pushed so the router's receiving socket gets the same. It stays below net.core.rmem_max (7.5 MB), past which the kernel silently clamps. tests/test_shipped_configs.sh now fails on a TCP template that pins a buffer, on a UDP one that asks for more than rmem_max, and on a UDP tun1 below 1 MB
    * shadowsocks.conf, shadowsocks.6.1.conf, shadowsocks.6.18.conf: raise the tcp_rmem/tcp_wmem maximum from 16 MB (32 MB in shadowsocks.conf) to 128 MB, what one connection needs for ~10 Gbit/s up to ~50 ms RTT: the sender needs about 2 x rate x RTT of buffer, so 16 MB held a single VPS-to-router download to roughly 3 Gbit/s at 20 ms and 1.3 Gbit/s at 50 ms, whatever the links. None of the proxies set their own socket buffers, so this is the limit that applied to all of them. omr-net-mem keeps the full 128 MB on any VPS with ~1 GB and lowers it on smaller ones so that no single socket's maximum can pass the tcp_mem pressure threshold (CONN_SHARE and SOCKET_BUFFERS in /etc/default/omr-net-mem); it only ever lowers the configured value and never goes below the kernel's own default. The cap is deliberately not divided by the MPTCP subflow count: an MPTCP connection has one connection-level receive window for all its subflows (mptcp_set_rwin(), mptcp_wnd_end()), and on the bench a three-subflow connection with its receiving application frozen held 98% of one tcp_rmem maximum, the subflows none; its send side is the sum of the subflows' buffers but those grow only with each path's real congestion window
    * omr-service: re-apply /etc/sysctl.d/99-omr-net-mem.conf after 90-shadowsocks.conf, so an omr restart cannot put back a socket buffer maximum the VPS's RAM does not allow
    * Add omr-net-mem and omr-net-mem.service: size net.ipv4.tcp_mem and net.ipv4.udp_mem from the RAM the VPS actually has, and stop shipping them in 90-shadowsocks.conf. Both count pages, and the fixed values sat far above the RAM of the VPSes they were installed on: on a 1 GB droplet shadowsocks.6.1.conf/6.18.conf put the TCP memory-pressure threshold at 3.2 GB and its ceiling at 6.4 GB (256 GB in shadowsocks.conf), and udp_mem "4096 87380 16777216" -- the classic tcp_rmem byte triple pasted into a page-count sysctl -- put the UDP ceiling at 64 GB. The kernel could therefore never apply memory pressure or refuse an allocation, and socket memory grew until the OOM killer fired, which on a VPS that terminates every router's proxy and VPN means one of those daemons. MPTCP makes the pool matter more: mptcp_prot.sysctl_mem is sysctl_tcp_mem, so the mptcp-level socket and every subflow draw on it. The sizing is the router's (16% of RAM for TCP from 768 MB, 12% from 192 MB, 8% below; 12% for UDP; the kernel's 3/4:1:3/2 shape; never below the kernel's own RAM/16 and RAM/8; refused above a 75%-of-RAM combined ceiling), overridable in /etc/default/omr-net-mem. The unit runs right after systemd-sysctl at every boot, so a resized VPS is re-sized, and the result is persisted to /etc/sysctl.d/99-omr-net-mem.conf. The templates must not set these keys again: omr-service re-applies 90-shadowsocks.conf with "sysctl -p" on every start. On the bench VPS (959 MB) that is tcp_mem 29482 39310 58965 and udp_mem 23033 30711 46066, and both survive omr-service's re-apply and a full systemd-sysctl run
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: install and enable omr-net-mem in both branches, and run it before the final service restarts so an updated VPS drops the old limits without waiting for a reboot. Add tests/test_net_mem.sh
    * Add omr-reserved-ports, omr-reserved-ports.service and omr-reserved-ports.path: keep the kernel's automatic port assignment off the ports the VPS's own services listen on. 90-shadowsocks.conf sets ip_local_port_range to 9999-65000, which contains the v2ray (10085) and xray (10086) API inbounds and, above all, the dokodemo-door inbounds omr-admin adds for every port a router forwards through a v2ray/xray proxy, on whatever port the user picked. Any outgoing connection or bind(0) could be handed one of them, and if it still held it when v2ray/xray restarted the daemon could not bind that inbound and failed to start altogether, taking every proxied router with it. The script reads the listen ports of v2ray, xray, shadowsocks-go, ss-manager, mqvpn and the per-user VXLAN tunnels (plus anything listed in /etc/openmptcprouter-vps-admin/reserved-ports) and writes them to net.ipv4.ip_local_reserved_ports, which automatic assignment skips while an explicit bind() still works; it refuses to reserve more than half of the range. The path unit re-runs it whenever one of those files changes -- a forwarded port added through omr-admin is reserved within a couple of seconds -- and the result is persisted to /etc/sysctl.d/99-omr-reserved-ports.conf, so it is in place from systemd-sysctl onwards and the service is ordered before the daemons it protects. The service has no start limit (StartLimitIntervalSec=0): a router sync rewrites several watched files in a row, 7 starts in 2 seconds on the test VPS, and systemd's default 5 per 10 seconds failed the service and, with it, the path unit, which then stopped watching until the next boot. Ports forwarded with DNAT need nothing: they are not listeners on the VPS
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: install and enable omr-reserved-ports in both the download and the LOCALFILES branches, and run it once before the install's final round of service restarts
    * Add tests/test_reserved_ports.sh (every source above, list folding, the half-range refusal, that the path unit watches every file the script reads, and that the service has no start limit); tests/test_systemd_units.sh now also parses .path.in units
    * omr-admin.service, omr-admin-ipv6.service: add CAP_SYS_MODULE to CapabilityBoundingSet. Enabling the SIP ALG makes omr-admin modprobe nf_conntrack_sip (the installer blacklists it, and a blacklist also stops the autoload when the ct helper object is created), and without the capability that modprobe failed with EPERM, leaving only a misleading ENOENT from the "add ct helper" lines; so SIP ALG could not be turned on from the router unless the module was already loaded
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: a missing omr-vps-admin deb no longer aborts the install. When neither the repository nor www.openmptcprouter.com/debian/ has the pinned OMR_ADMIN_BINARY_VERSION (bumped here before the deb is uploaded), the wget 404 ended the whole run under set -e; it now warns and installs the repository's newest omr-vps-admin instead, so an update keeps the installed one and a fresh VPS still gets an admin API
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: when this release's omr-server deb is not in the repository (a rolling-test not published yet), do not try to install it and remove the omr-server package left installed by an earlier version, which claimed a version the VPS does not run and kept an older copy of the installer in /usr/share/omr-server. A failed install of a version the repository does have still only warns, as before
    * nftables/omr.nft: accept DHCPv6 replies and MLD queries again (#4381), both accepted by Shorewall6 and both lost in the 0.1068 switch to nftables. A DHCPv6 Solicit goes to ff02::1:2 and the Advertise/Reply comes back from the server's link-local address to ours, which conntrack does not see as the reply to the multicast Solicit, so it was NEW, nothing accepted udp/546, and the trailing reject answered it with a port unreachable: a VPS that takes its IPv6 address by DHCPv6 never got one, and one that already had a lease lost it at the first Renew. MLD is left untracked by conntrack and only NS/RA/NA were accepted, so queries were rejected and never answered, and behind a switch doing MLD snooping the VPS's solicited-node group expired, the router's neighbour solicitations stopped reaching it and IPv6 to the VPS went away with the router's neighbour entry. Now "ip6 daddr fe80::/10 udp dport 546 accept" (shorewall6/interfaces "dhcp") and "ip6 saddr fe80::/10 icmpv6 type mld-listener-query accept" (shorewall6.conf AllowICMPs); neither is tied to $NET_IFACE, IPv6 not always being on it (#3271), the link-local address keeping both on the link. The MLD rule must not start with "ip6 nexthdr icmpv6" like the ND one: MLD carries a hop-by-hop header, so that rule loads and never matches. Checked on the test VPS with the issue's netns reproducer (dnsmasq and dhclient -6, a scapy MLDv2 query): no lease and no report before, a lease and an MLDv2 report after, and udp/546 to a global address still rejected. tests/test_nftables.sh now fails when either rule is missing or the MLD rule matches on nexthdr
    * nftables: give the router its IPv6 back (#4382), lost in the 0.1068 switch to nftables. VPN_IFACES was taken from shorewall4/interfaces and so lacked omr-6in4-user*, the sit tunnel the router's IPv6 comes through, which shorewall6/interfaces had in the vpn zone: everything the router routed into it matched nothing in the forward chain and got a port unreachable from fd00::a00:1, so the LAN had no IPv6 to the internet (ULA or a public "IPv6 Prefix"), IPv6 port redirects to fd00::a00:2 were rejected, and the router reached the VPS over the tunnel only on ports open to the internet. omr-6in4-user* is in VPN_IFACES now. That alone would have opened the router's LAN to the internet whenever it uses a public prefix, which omr-6in4-run routes into the tunnel, so the IPv6 net->vpn forward rule only accepts connections user_dnat redirected ("ct status dnat"), as shorewall6's net all DROP plus DNAT rules did; IPv4 keeps its rule. Also bring back INTERFACE6 (#3271), computed by the installer and no longer used since 0.1068: omr-vars.nft has a NET_IFACE6, the installer writes $INTERFACE6 there (the $INTERFACE sed skips that line, so an IPv6 NIC really named eth0 is not rewritten), and the NAT66 masquerade and the IPv6 net->vpn rule use it, so on a VPS with IPv6 on another NIC the LAN's ULA traffic is masqueraded and IPv6 redirects arriving there are forwarded. Checked on the test VPS with the issue's netns reproducer, one NIC and IPv6 on a second NIC: before, ping6 from the LAN, the public prefix, the IPv6 redirect and router->VPS over the tunnel all rejected; after, all four work, leaving through the IPv6 NIC masqueraded, and unsolicited TCP from the internet to the LAN's public address is still rejected. tests/test_nftables.sh now fails when omr-6in4-user* leaves VPN_IFACES, when IPv6 net->vpn is accepted without ct status dnat, when the NAT66 masquerade is not on $NET_IFACE6, and runs the installer's sed lines against omr-vars.nft for four INTERFACE/INTERFACE6 pairs
    * Update omr-admin to 84b20c1 (0.18+20261004): load nf_conntrack_sip before creating the SIP ct helpers and log why when it cannot, fix SIP nft rules, fix multi users backup, code clean-up, add omr-6in4-user* to the VPN interfaces of the nftables rules omr-admin renders (#4382)

 -- OpenMPTCProuter <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0200

omr-server (0.1084) unstable; urgency=medium

    * multipath: announce only the addresses a remote peer can actually reach. The on, signal, backup and handover branches read every address of the device ("ip -j a show $DEVICE | jq -r '.[0].addr_info[].local'") and added one MPTCP endpoint per address, so a VPS whose NIC carries more than its public address told every router to open subflows to addresses that only exist inside the datacentre: on a DigitalOcean droplet eth0 has the public IPv4, the private anchor address (10.x/16) and an IPv6 link-local, and all three were signalled. The failure hides well, the anchor address answering ping and TCP from a router because those go through the VPN tunnel, while an MP_JOIN does not -- it is bound to a WAN, and from there the address is unreachable. Measured on the bench, every socket in SYN-SENT during a transfer was "192.168.200.79%eth2 -> 10.19.0.6:65280", 6 of every 10 MP_JOIN SYNs unanswered, 50622 sent against 7440 answered since boot, and the dead subflows sat in the list for hours holding slots that real WANs needed. The jq and jsonfilter expressions now select scope "global", which drops link-local and loopback outright, and the new omr_filter_endpoint_ips() drops the RFC1918, CGNAT and ULA addresses as well, but only when the device also carries a public address of the same family, so a VPS genuinely behind NAT still announces the only address it has. The endpoint list is not persistent, so it is rebuilt from the filtered set at the next boot; on a running VPS omr-service re-runs the command only when the device is not already in the mode it wants, so an endpoint added by an earlier version stays until then
    * multipath: clear every endpoint of the device and not just the first one. "ID=$(ip mptcp endpoint show | grep -m 1 "dev $DEVICE" | awk '{print $3}')" yielded a single id while the off, on, signal and backup branches all loop over $ID to empty the device before adding its addresses again, so a device carrying more than one endpoint -- which is every dual-stack eth0 -- was never emptied: one endpoint went and the rest stayed. "multipath <dev> off" therefore could not turn a device off at all, and the three branches that re-add addresses were left relying on "ip mptcp endpoint add" refusing a duplicate to come out right. Without the -m 1 the correction above reaches a VPS that is already up, the endpoints this version stops announcing being removed the next time the device's mode is set rather than surviving it. IFF keeps its -m 1, that one only reads the flags back for display
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: MPTCP_BPF_VERSION 1.0-1 becomes 1.2-1, the version all eight mptcp-bpf-* scheduler packages are installed at on KERNEL=6.18. The old pin had stopped meaning anything: apt carries no 1.0-1 any more, so each of the eight fell through to the wget fallback on every run ("E: Version '1.0-1' for 'mptcp-bpf-weight' was not found"), and the file published as mptcp-bpf-weight_1.0-1_amd64.deb holds version 1.1-1, which dpkg duly unpacked "(1.1-1) over (1.1-1)" -- the install read as pinning a version while installing whatever that filename happened to contain. Checked against the download server: all eight are published at 1.2-1, and mptcp-bpf-weight_1.2-1_amd64.deb really does declare 1.2-1
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: stopping a service can no longer end the install. Sixteen blocks follow the shape "if systemctl -q is-active X; then systemctl -q stop X > /dev/null 2>&1; systemctl -q disable X > /dev/null 2>&1; fi", and while the is-active test is an if condition and so exempt from set -e, the two commands in the body are not. "systemctl stop" returns non-zero when its job is superseded or cancelled, which is exactly what happens when something else restarts the unit in the same moment, and omr-admin's POST /mptcp restarts the very units these blocks stop (shadowsocks-libev, shadowsocks-go, v2ray, xray, glorytun-tcp, openvpn) -- an endpoint a router pushes whenever its MPTCP settings change. An install running while a router is attached could therefore die at any of the sixteen, silently: the EXIT trap removes /run/omr-install.running on the way out, so all that is left is a log that stops mid-section and a half configured VPS. Caught on the test VPS, where a run ended straight after the OBFS step in the same second the router POSTed /mptcp while an identical re-run minutes later went through to the end. The 28 stop and disable calls carry "|| true" now, which is the treatment the omr-admin pair at the top of the file already had. The window had just widened: omr-vps-admin 0.18+20260923, pinned above, is what added shadowsocks-go to that restart list
    * Update omr-admin to 0.18+20260924: fix the router failing to open a port whenever LuCI's "Restrict to address family" is left at "IPv4 and IPv6". That stores family='any' in uci and the router's _vps_firewall_redirect_port hands the value straight to the API, but the IPPROTO enum knew ipv4 and ipv6 only, so the POST came back 422 Unprocessable Content, the port was never opened and nothing noticed, the router never looking at the result of _set_json. 'any' now means both families on /shorewall, /shorewalllist, /shorewallopen and /shorewallclose (and their /firewall* names): open writes a v4 and a v6 rule, close removes both, the bulk redirect toggles move together, and the list returns both families deduplicated, the legacy line layout the router greps having no family column. An 'any' rule that also restricts a source address stays on the family of that literal, a v6 copy of an IPv4 restriction rendering "meta nfproto ipv6 ip6 daddr 1.2.3.4", which nft refuses -- and since user_accept and user_dnat are flushed in a single nft transaction, that one rule would have taken every opened port of every user down with it
    * Update omr-admin: fix a scheduler pushed by the router reaching the upload direction only on a VPS where the proxy is shadowsocks-go, which is the default for shadowsocks-rust and -go routers. An MPTCP socket keeps the scheduler its listener had when it was created, so writing net.mptcp.scheduler into 90-shadowsocks.conf never reaches the listeners already running, which is why POST /mptcp restarts every service that terminates MPTCP once that file really changed; shadowsocks-libev, v2ray, xray, glorytun-tcp and openvpn were on that list and shadowsocks-go never was. A router selecting bpf_red for redundancy therefore got it on everything it sent and silently kept the previous scheduler on everything the server sent back, until the next unrelated restart of shadowsocks-go
    * Update omr-admin: omr-admin-config.json is no longer read, edited and json.dump'ed back over itself with no lock. omr-admin runs two uvicorn workers plus the metrics engine's background threads while the router pushes several endpoints in the same second, so two overlapping requests meant the second write silently reverted the first, and a reader or a crash landing in the middle of the dump saw a half-written file that omr-admin then refused to start on. Every mutation goes through one helper that takes an exclusive flock, re-reads the config under it and replaces the file by temp file plus fsync plus rename; /add_user and /remove_user hold that lock for the whole request, having spent seconds generating tunnel material between their read and their write; readers take it shared; and install_omr-ai.sh and the metrics engine's auto_learning toggle use the same lock rather than racing the API
    * Update omr-admin: GET /status answered 500 for a username that is not in the config (the unknown-user dict was passed on as a userid, which resolved to an empty username, which died on a KeyError after the whole VPS status had been collected), and POST /backuppost opened both destination files for writing before base64-decoding the body, so a truncated upload left the user with no backup at all. Both are checked before anything is opened or indexed now
    * debian/control: follow the pin above, omr-vps-admin (>= 0.18+20260910) becoming (>= 0.18+20260924). A pin that does not match the version the installer asks apt for is what 0.1082 fixed across the whole list, and one unresolvable entry is enough for the install's last step to leave the VPS with no omr-server package at all

 -- OpenMPTCProuter <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Thu, 24 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200

omr-server (0.1083) unstable; urgency=medium

    * omr-service: _vpn1 removed vpn1's default route and put nothing back. It deleted the route from the main table first, then ran "ip r a default via $vpngw dev vpn1 table 991337" -- $vpngw is set nowhere in this repository; the line just above it computes $vpn1gw, and even that one takes awk's $3 out of "echo \"$vpn1route\"", whose literal quotes shift every field by one. The loop that was meant to copy the remaining routes, "for route in $(ip r show dev vpn1)", splits each route into words rather than lines, so what it actually ran was "ip r a default table 991337", "ip r a via table 991337", "ip r a 192.0.2.1 table 991337" and so on, one command per word. The function is called from the omr-service loop every 10 seconds, so on a VPS that has a vpn1 interface the first pass took its default route away for good, on both tables. It now reads "ip -4 route show dev vpn1" line by line, copies each route whole into table 991337 with "ip -4 route replace", and only then deletes the main-table default, returning without deleting anything if a copy fails: the worst case is a policy table that is not populated yet rather than an interface with no way out
    * omr-service: the ten "if systemctl list-unit-files <unit> >/dev/null" guards were true whether or not the unit existed. list-unit-files reports whether the query worked, not whether anything matched: given a pattern nothing matches it prints "0 unit files listed." and exits 0. So the glorytun-udp, glorytun-tcp, dsvpn, softether, shadowsocks-libev, shadowsocks-go, xray, v2ray, wireguard and mqvpn blocks all ran on every VPS regardless of what is installed, each logging "<x> not detected, restart <x>" and asking systemd to restart a unit it does not have, for ever. A VPS using one tunnel wrote nine of those lines per pass into the journal, which is also what made a real "not detected" impossible to notice. The new _unit_exists helper requires the listing to actually name something
    * omr-service: derive the client end of the tunnel from the last octet only. "echo $localip | sed 's/\.1/\.2/'" rewrites the first ".1" anywhere in the address, which is the last octet only as long as nothing earlier in the address contains one: the defaults 10.255.255.1 (glorytun-tcp) and 10.255.251.1 (dsvpn) are safe, a LOCALIP of 10.1.0.1 in /etc/glorytun-tcp/tun0 or a LOCALTUNIP of 192.168.1.1 in /etc/dsvpn/dsvpn0 becomes 10.2.0.1 and 192.268.1.1. The health check then pings an address that has nothing to do with the tunnel, gets 100% loss, and both functions conclude the client end is gone and restart a tunnel that was working. The substitution is anchored to the end of the address now
    * omr-pihole.sh: same guard, "systemctl list-unit-files lighttpd.service &>/dev/null && systemctl -q restart lighttpd", so the restart was attempted on every run, including on a Pi-hole install that does not use lighttpd, where it prints a systemd error in the middle of the script's own output. "&>" is a bashism in a "#!/bin/sh" script as well. Also give the file the final newline it never had
    * omr-bypass: write the checksum of omr-bypass.json into omr-admin-config.json once the ipsets, both firewall rulesets and the routing are in place, instead of as the script's first act. Recording the new list as applied before applying any of it means anything that fails afterwards leaves the VPS with the old bypass rules and a checksum saying they are current, so every following run stops at "[ "$CHECKSUM" = "$PREVIOUS_CHECKSUM" ] && exit 0" -- and the only thing that runs this script is omr-bypass.timer, every 300s, so the router's bypass list quietly never took effect until the user changed it again. With "set -e" now at the top, a failure ends the run before the checksum is written and the next tick retries it
    * omr-bypass: read the per-interface address lists with "jq -r --arg intf ... '.[$intf].ipv4[]?'" rather than interpolating the interface name into the filter as ".${INTERFACE}.ipv4[]". That name comes from bypass_intf in omr-admin-config.json, i.e. from the router, and anything that is not a bare identifier is a jq syntax error rather than a lookup (eth0.100, ens3-1), so the whole filter failed to compile and the interface got no addresses at all. The trailing "?" makes an interface with no ipv4 or no ipv6 key an empty list instead of an error, which matters now that set -e is on; for the same reason the two "ipset -q flush" calls, which fail when the set does not exist yet, are guarded by an "ipset -q list" first
    * omr-bypass: the IPv6 half never had an IPv6 route. The block under "if [ -n "$GATEWAY6" ]" ran "ip rule add" and "ip route replace default via ${GATEWAY6} dev ${INTERFACE} table ${TABLE}" without -6, so it added a second copy of the same IPv4 rule and handed an IPv6 gateway to the IPv4 route table, where it can only fail -- and the failure went unseen, both commands having their output sent to /dev/null. The ip6tables chains above it were therefore marking packets for a table with no default route in it and no rule pointing at it, so the traffic that was supposed to bypass the tunnel took the tunnel. Both commands carry -6 now, the rules are deleted before being added rather than accumulating a duplicate at priority 1 on every change, and only the delete is allowed to fail
    * omr-update: the full reinstall path fetched the installer over plain http and piped it into a root shell, "wget -O - http://www.openmptcprouter.com/server/debian.sh | sh", then removed /etc/openmptcprouter-vps-admin/update and rebooted on the next two lines no matter what happened. A pipeline's status is the last command's, so an empty body from a failed download is an empty script that exits 0: the flag was cleared and the machine rebooted having installed nothing, and the router kept offering an update that never applied. It now downloads over https into a temporary file, runs it only if the download completed, and clears the flag and reboots only if both steps succeeded; otherwise it says which step failed and keeps the flag so the next run retries. This is the treatment 0.1082 gave the update-bin path, which is the other half of the same unit
    * docs/USER_MANUAL.md: the install command documented there is https now, not http, the answer being piped straight into a root shell. It keeps "| sh": a piped script never sees its own shebang, so the interpreter named on the command line is the one that runs it, and the installer is #!/bin/sh with no bashisms in it. Under bash the six "echo \"\033[1m...\033[0m\"" lines print their escapes literally instead of rendering, among them the line that shows the generated omr-admin password and the one telling the user which port sshd moved to. omr-update runs the file it downloads with sh for the same reason
    * Add docs/TECHNICAL.md: what this repository owns and what lives in the sibling repositories, the installer's structure and conventions (env-var configuration, the symlinked per-distribution entry points, set -e), the contract a tunnel runner script and its unit template follow, omr-service, the fail2ban filters and jails, the Debian packaging and its CI, the nftables ruleset after the Shorewall migration, and what to check before shipping a change
    * Add tests/test_runtime_regressions.sh, covering the runtime paths above with the networking commands replaced by mocks, none of which needs a VPS: _vpn1 copies each route as one route and deletes the main-table default only after the copies are in, and deletes nothing when a copy fails; _unit_exists reads an empty listing as absent and a listed unit as present; omr-update no longer pipes a download into a shell and fetches over https; omr-bypass writes its checksum after the IPv6 rule and route. tests/run_all.sh picks it up on its own

 -- OpenMPTCProuter <contact@openmptcprouter.com>  Wed, 16 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0200

omr-server (0.1082) unstable; urgency=medium

    * debian/control: bring the omr-server dependencies back in line with the versions the installer actually asks apt for. Every pin in that list had drifted (omr-shadowsocks-libev 3.3.5-2 vs 3.3.5-3, omr-mlvpn 3.0.0+20201216.git.2263bab vs 3.0.0+20211028.git.ddafba3, omr-glorytun 0.3.4-4 vs 0.3.4-5, omr-glorytun-tcp 0.0.35-3 vs 0.0.35-6, v2ray 4.35.1 vs 5.32.0, omr-vps-admin 0.18+20260824 vs 0.18+20260910), and a single unresolvable pin is enough to make the last step of the install, "apt-get -y install omr-server=${OMR_VERSION} >/dev/null 2>&1 || true", do nothing at all without a word in the log, leaving the VPS with no omr-server package and no version marker. Added the components the installer has gained since the list was last touched: xray, shadowsocks-go and mqvpn
    * debian/control: drop three dependencies that cannot be satisfied or are actively wrong. linux-image-5.4.100-mptcp (= 1.18+9d3f35b) is a kernel no setting installs any more (KERNEL=5.4 now means 5.4.207-mptcp 1.22, the default KERNEL=6.18 is a PSABI-specific .deb fetched by wget and dpkg whose name cannot be written down at packaging time); omr-iperf3 is removed by the installer itself on amd64, so apt would have taken omr-server down with it; libcurl4 does not exist on Debian 13, which is exactly why the installer stopped installing it there. iptables goes too, the ruleset being nftables since 0.1068
    * debian/control: move to Recommends what the installer only installs under a condition (tracebox, amd64 only; iperf3, IPERF=yes) and to Suggests what is off by default (v2ray-plugin, V2RAY_PLUGIN=no), so a VPS installed with a feature turned off is not handed it back by dependency resolution. Also give the package the extended description it never had
    * debian/rules: ship the systemd/ directory, missing from the package since it was added in 0.1080. override_dh_auto_install sweeps the repository root file by file and then copies each subdirectory by name (shorewall4, shorewall6, nftables, bin), so a new top level directory is invisible to it: /usr/share/omr-server/systemd/20-omr-wait-online-any.conf never existed, and the LOCALFILES branch copies it unguarded ("cp ${DIR}/systemd/20-omr-wait-online-any.conf ..."), which under set -e ended every omr-update run right there, halfway through the firewall section. omr-update is exactly the LOCALFILES=yes caller ("LOCALFILES=yes SOURCES=yes REINSTALL=no /usr/share/omr-server/debian9-x86_64.sh"), so this hit the automatic update path only, which is where it would have been hardest to see
    * debian/rules: replace the file sweep's two -iname exclusions with -maxdepth 1. -iname matches a basename, never a path, so '*/debian/*' and '*/.git/*' excluded nothing and every file of .git and debian/ was handed to cp with a destination directory that does not exist. The package contents are unchanged (those copies all failed, find ignores the exit status of -exec), but a build no longer prints several hundred cp errors, and packaging internals can no longer end up shipped by accident if one of those directories ever gets created first
    * Add .github/workflows/build-deb.yml, building the omr-server package on push to develop and master, on every pull request, on v* tags and on demand. It stamps the changelog with OMR_VERSION before building, the version the installer's own "apt-get -y install omr-server=${OMR_VERSION}" asks for, since a package built from debian/changelog alone carries the bare 0.1081 and can never answer a request for 0.1081-rolling-test. It then runs tests/test_deb_contents.sh against the artifact, lintian informationally, uploads the .deb, .buildinfo and .changes as a build artifact, and on a tag attaches the .deb to that tag's release. Publishing to repo.openmptcprouter.com is not part of it, the repository holds no credentials for that
    * Add tests/test_deb_contents.sh, which checks a built package against the installer it ships: every ${DIR} path the installer reads is present (the systemd/ miss above), nothing from .git or debian/ leaked in, each version pin in debian/control equals the *_VERSION constant it mirrors (the drift fixed above), the dependencies dropped in this version stay dropped, and the package version equals OMR_VERSION
    * Add tests/test_installer_refs.sh, which checks debian9-x86_64.sh against the repository and against debian/ on a plain checkout, no build and no VPS involved: every file it reads exists (a wget of a renamed file exits non-zero and ends the install on a half configured machine), the LOCALFILES=no and LOCALFILES=yes branches install the same set of files (the 6.18 sysctl set was missing from the second one in 0.1080, so every update re-installed the 6.1 set on a 6.18 kernel), every subdirectory it reads is copied by debian/rules (the systemd/ miss of 0.1081), every pin in debian/control equals the *_VERSION constant it mirrors, OMR_VERSION and the newest changelog entry agree, and debian/postinst's two ^-anchored rewrites still match a line in the installer
    * Add tests/test_shipped_configs.sh: shell syntax of every shipped script, every symlink the download server serves resolves, every JSON template parses, every literal "sed -i s/X/.../" placeholder exists in the template behind the file it edits (a miss leaves the placeholder in the deployed config, e.g. XRAY_PSK as the actual key), every inbound tag the installer selects with jq is declared by a template (a renamed tag silently adds no user), the templates the runtime appends to end with a newline (0.1080), no CRLF, and the sysctl sets parse with no key given two different values
    * Add tests/test_fail2ban.sh, exercising the shipped filters against journal lines of both kinds. A filter fails silently in both directions: too loose and it bans a router out of the API it is being set up from (the /token and /login_basic false positives of 0.1079, and an unanchored regex reading an access log lets the request path forge a match and ban any address the attacker names), too tight or aimed at the wrong _daemon and it matches nothing while looking exactly like "no attacks". Also checks that every shipped filter is installed by both install branches under the name the jail asks for, that each jail names a filter that exists and a journalmatch unit the installer sets up, that loopback stays in ignoreip (omr-service probes the API on 127.0.0.1 every 10s) and that the omradmin jail stays disabled. common.conf is not available outside a fail2ban install, so its __prefix_line is approximated with the shape the systemd backend feeds a filter
    * Add tests/test_systemd_units.sh: every unit, drop-in and .network file parses, template units use their instance specifier and non-template ones do not (a %i there expands to nothing), every Exec= path this repository ships is a path the installer actually writes (otherwise the unit fails at every start with 203/EXEC), drop-ins clear ExecStart before replacing it, the wait-online drop-in keeps --any, -o routable and --timeout, the nftables drop-in keeps its --no-block try-restart of omr-admin (a blocking one deadlocks against After=nftables.service), omr-admin.service stays ordered after nftables.service, and every unit the installer copies into /lib/systemd/system is enabled or started by something
    * Add tests/test_nftables.sh: the includes exist and are installed where nftables.conf expects them, every $variable used in omr.nft is defined, every jump target is a declared chain, the six chains omradmin.py writes into by name (user_accept, user_dnat, gre_snat, client2client, dscp_mark, ct_helpers) are still there, every base chain declares its hook, and the two rules with a history hold: the output chain rejects fw->vpncl only and not "everything that is not $NET_IFACE" (which takes DNS away from a VPS whose resolver sits on a second NIC), and the input chain accepts the port the installer moves sshd to. Since /etc/nftables.conf starts with "flush ruleset", anything unparseable here takes the whole firewall down at boot, on a machine nobody is watching. Runs "nft -c -f" on top of all that when nft is installed and permitted, and reports it as skipped otherwise
    * Add tests/run_all.sh, which runs every tests/test_*.sh and counts a test that cannot run for want of its subject as skipped rather than passed, so "0 failed" never quietly means "nothing ran". tests/test_deb_contents.sh now exits 77 (skipped) instead of failing when no .deb has been built
    * Add .github/workflows/tests.yml, running the suite on push to develop and master, on every pull request, on v* tags and on demand, plus a second pass of the nftables test as root for a real "nft -c -f" parse (checking a ruleset needs CAP_NET_ADMIN) and an informational shellcheck
    * Remove the kernel .deb from /tmp once dpkg has installed it, in all four kernel branches. /tmp is a tmpfs on Debian 13, so those files are RAM, not disk: 95 MB for the image plus 10 MB for the headers, held until the next reboot, on a VPS that may only have 1 GB. Measured on a test VPS: 105 MB of /tmp out of 959 MB total, which left apt-get without the ~150 MB it needs to build its package cache -- "apt-get install" was OOM-killed three times in a row there, and completed on the first try after those two files were deleted, with no swap added. Every other download into /tmp was already cleaned up; the largest one was not. The remaining stragglers go too (EasyRSA and glorytun tarballs, the vps-admin zip, the Python 3.7 tarball, update-grub.sh)
    * The last step of the install, "apt-get -y install omr-server=${OMR_VERSION}", now reports what went wrong instead of being silenced with ">/dev/null 2>&1 || true". When it does not install, the VPS is left with no omr-server package and no version marker, and the two ways that happens are indistinguishable from the outside: a version apt cannot resolve (a pin in debian/control that drifted, or a version never published) and apt being OOM-killed, which the kernel .debs above used to cause. Both were happening on the test VPS
    * omr-update: wait for apt/dpkg to be done before running the installer. This unit is started by the omr-server postinst, i.e. from inside the apt run that is installing that very package, and the installer gives up a few lines in when the dpkg frontend lock is still held ("E: Unable to acquire the dpkg frontend lock ... process 1926128 (apt-get)" then "E: `apt-get check` failed, you may have broken packages. Aborting..."), which took one second and removed the update flag on the way out -- the update was lost with nothing but two lines in a log nobody reads. It also keeps /etc/openmptcprouter-vps-admin/update-bin when the installer exits non-zero, so a failed update is retried instead of silently dropped
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: exit 0, with a line saying so, when REINSTALL=no and the VPS already runs this version. That path exited 1, which is what omr-update above would now read as a failed run and retry for ever. Nothing to do is not an error
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: mark a run in progress in /run/omr-install.running (removed by a trap on exit; /run is a tmpfs, so a lock left by a killed run is gone at the next boot). The omr-server postinst checks it before asking omr-update for a run: this script ends by installing that package, so on a fresh install of a published version it would otherwise start a second copy of itself on top of the first
    * Write /etc/openvpn/tun0.conf with a rename instead of in place, in both install branches. omr-admin rewrites that same file itself (the client-to-client sync, and once at every startup, which this script triggers when it restarts omr-admin): it reads the file line by line and moves its own copy over the result, so a read landing inside our write loses the file. Caught on the test VPS during an update run -- wget logged "'/etc/openvpn/tun0.conf' saved [770/770]" at 14:08:29 and the file was 0 bytes one second later, after which openvpn@tun0 failed every restart with "Options error: You must define TUN/TAP device (--dev)" and the tunnel stayed down with nothing else to show for it. A rename is atomic, so omr-admin sees either the old file or the new one. This became easy to hit now that installing the package runs the installer while omr-admin is live
    * debian/rules: build both archive members with xz explicitly (dh_builddeb -- -Zxz). dpkg-deb's default compression follows the distribution doing the build, and .github/workflows/build-deb.yml builds on ubuntu-latest, whose dpkg writes zstd: reprepro reads gzip and xz only, so such a package is refused with "Could not find a suitable control.tar file within '...omr-server_0.1082_all.deb'!" and never makes it into the repository that the installer's last step, "apt-get -y install omr-server=${OMR_VERSION}", pulls from -- while the same source built on Debian went in without a word. An existing zstd package can be repacked without a rebuild: "dpkg-deb -R x.deb tmpdir && fakeroot dpkg-deb -Zxz --build tmpdir x.deb"
    * tests: every file the installer downloads into /tmp is removed again (the leak above), debian/rules writes only inside the package staging directory, the installer marks its run with the lock the postinst reads, and omr-update waits for the package manager and keeps its flag when the installer fails
    * Fix installing or upgrading the omr-server package doing nothing on the VPS beyond refreshing /usr/share/omr-server. debian/postinst has always ended with "systemctl restart omr-update", and omr-update re-runs the installer only when /etc/openmptcprouter-vps-admin/update-bin exists -- the flag debian/rules meant to ship, except it touched it into $(CURDIR)/debian/etc/openmptcprouter-vps-admin/ while the package stages under $(CURDIR)/debian/omr-server/, so it was in no package at all and that restart has been starting a unit that exited in the same second ever since df637bb. Verified on a test VPS: before this change the built .deb carried nothing under /etc and the postinst's omr-update run lasted under a second; after it, the install triggers a complete installer run (4m34s there) that re-applies every file, restarts what changed and updates the version in the motd, which is where omr-admin reads vps.omr_version for the router
    * debian/postinst now creates that flag itself rather than shipping it: dpkg flags everything under /etc as a conffile, omr-update removes the flag when the run is done, and a conffile deleted on the box is not restored by a later upgrade (checked here, 0.1082 -> 0.1083: it stayed gone), so a shipped flag would have armed the update on the very first install and never again
    * debian/postinst: skip the restart while omr-update is already running, and never fail the package install because of it. The installer that run executes ends with "apt-get -y install omr-server=${OMR_VERSION}", which can bring this same script back on a version change -- restarting omr-update from there would kill the install halfway through its own run. The restart is also no longer fatal (the unit can be masked or missing, and under "#!/bin/sh -e" that ended the postinst with an error and left the package half-configured, seen while testing); the flag stays on disk either way, so the next trigger picks it up
    * tests/test_deb_contents.sh: check the update-bin flag end of this -- the postinst creates it, restarts omr-update, guards against doing that inside a running update, and the package declares no conffiles. tests/test_installer_refs.sh: check every $(CURDIR)/debian/ path in debian/rules is under the package staging directory, which is the bug class above
    * shadowsocks.6.1.conf: drop the five out of tree net.mptcp.mptcp_* keys, the same cleanup 0.1080 did for shadowsocks.6.18.conf. That file is installed for KERNEL=6.6 and KERNEL=6.12, both mainline MPTCP kernels, where the whole net.mptcp.mptcp_* namespace (checksum, path_manager, scheduler, syn_retries, version) does not exist: it produced a systemd-sysctl warning per boot and set nothing. Only shadowsocks.conf (KERNEL=5.4, the out of tree v0.95 kernel) legitimately carries them. Also drop the duplicate "net.mptcp.checksum_enabled=0" that both 6.1 and 6.18 carried a second time at the end of the file, next to the block omr-admin appends: same value, so nothing was ever wrong with it, but a second copy is one more place for the two to drift apart until the router's first settings push rewrites every net.mptcp line anyway
    * fail2ban filter shadowsocks-go: match the failed handshake wherever it sits on the journal line. The regex required the message to follow the daemon prefix immediately, so it matched only zap's systemd/console preset with no timestamp of its own -- under the json preset ({"level":"warn","msg":"Failed to complete handshake with client",...}) or a console line carrying its own timestamp it matched nothing at all, and a jail that bans no one is indistinguishable from an absence of attacks. The address is still read only from an unescaped "clientAddress": field, which text coming from the client cannot forge: zap escapes every quote inside a field value
    * omr-update: stop passing LOCALFILES=yes SOURCES=yes to /usr/share/omr-server/debian9-x86_64.sh, which never selected anything. debian/postinst rewrites that copy's own "LOCALFILES=${LOCALFILES:-no}" and "SOURCES=${SOURCES:-no}" into plain "no" (it has done so since the first Debian packaging commit), and a bare assignment beats the environment, so the update has always taken the download branch rather than reading /usr/share/omr-server or building from a git clone. Behaviour is unchanged, the line now says what actually happens, and the comment explains why the two variables are not there. REINSTALL=no is kept: nothing rewrites that one
    * debian9-x86_64.sh: drop "sed -i s:XRAY_UUID:...:g /etc/xray/xray-server.json", which never replaced anything -- that template carries V2RAY_UUID, substituted with the same value by the line right after it. xray-vless-reality.json is the file that really holds an XRAY_UUID placeholder and keeps its own substitution

